Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) Queue Implementation

This implementation of an SPSC (Single-Producer Single-Consumer) queue provides a bounded, lock-free ring buffer intended for low-latency inter-thread communication.
The queue uses preallocated storage, cache-line separation for producer and consumer indices, and acquire-release memory ordering to achieve predictable performance without locks, heap allocations, or kernel involvement.

When instantiated with pointer types, the queue supports zero-copy data transfer, allowing ownership of payload objects to move between threads without copying the underlying data.

Properties and Advantages

Single-Producer Single-Consumer semantics
Designed specifically for one producer thread and one consumer thread, allowing a simpler and more efficient synchronization model than general MPMC queues.

Lock-free operation
Uses atomic operations and memory ordering instead of mutexes, avoiding contention, priority inversion, and system calls.

Bounded ring buffer
Fixed capacity with preallocated storage ensures deterministic memory usage and predictable behavior under load.

Low-latency behavior

No dynamic memory allocation on the hot path

No locks or blocking calls

Minimal atomic operations per enqueue/dequeue

Cache-line-aligned head and tail indices to reduce false sharing

Zero-copy semantics (pointer usage)
When the queue stores pointer types (e.g., Order*), payload objects are never copied. Only pointer values are transferred, enabling efficient ownership handoff between producer and consumer.

Busy-spin waiting model
The producer spins when the queue is full, favoring low latency and low variance over CPU efficiency. This makes the queue suitable for dedicated-thread, latency-sensitive workloads.

Cache-friendly design
Sequential access to the ring buffer and separation of producer and consumer indices improve cache locality.

Deterministic performance characteristics
Fixed memory footprint and bounded operations make the queue suitable for real-time or near-real-time systems where predictable latency is important.
